{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1758 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "a236add0034884310b4afc181d4289f079d967c72b5d14294c317024d21b37ee" :edb/db-before-t 1757 :edb/db-after-t 1758 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535070 50 #inst "2026-10-08T16:12:41.919Z" 13194139535070 true] [17592186105615 1100 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $x^2+y^2 = 2$. Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$." 13194139535070 false] [17592186105615 1100 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $x^2+y^2 = 2.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" 13194139535070 true] [17592186105615 1145 "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/0c0a4b177a997ffb38489abc902a1c78d027d0da661b1498fc97d3d9a77862d9.png)\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/0cd188ffe544d454a3375df5f043096888f6dfd5243551d35502efda4bcfeb11.png)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/5f0d414a5388ae374f46f75825b9ffec7725649a97a45de24f8178d95a94b1ad.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2}\\sin t)^2 + (\\sqrt{2}\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\cos{t}$ and $y(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\sin{t},$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535070 true] [17592186105615 1145 "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$.\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}x^{′}(t) \\\\ y^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2}\\sin t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\cos t\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\mathbf r'(t) \\mid \\mid = \\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2}\\sin t)^2 + (\\sqrt{2}\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nand since for our curve\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\cos{t}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\sin{t}\n$$\n\nit follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= (1)/(\\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid)\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\cos t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\sin t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\cos t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\sin t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535070 false] [17592186105616 1100 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf r(t) =  12\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 9\\cos t \\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0,2\\pi).$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" 13194139535070 true] [17592186105616 1100 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf r(t) = 12\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 9\\cos t \\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $[MATH: t \\in [0,2\\pi).]$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$.\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" 13194139535070 false] [17592186105616 1145 "For convenience, we express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/007f19fb0c6e24115d719178a4b44e78c9881b9f71f52d0f2970480358e3faab.png)\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/ec0ddbbff34ed096bb872f23663f97005932a4ea8f368059cc4b511a5c9c76cc.png)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/6f74bda044d15ed504a0b95b1e60ae8542f2b304f1e2712ae8a1d840a45aeaad.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{\\sqrt{144{cos}^{2}⁡t+81{sin}^{2}⁡t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535070 true] [17592186105616 1145 "For convenience, we express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$.\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}x^{′}(t) \\\\ y^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}12\\cos t \\\\ -9\\sin t\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= (1)/(\\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid)\\begin{bmatrix}9\\sin t \\\\ 12\\cos t\\end{bmatrix} = \\frac{9\\sin ti + 12\\cos tj}{\\sqrt{144\\cos^{2} t + 81\\sin^{2} t}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535070 false] [17592186105776 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329644 13194139535070 true] [17592186105776 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329651 13194139535070 true] [17592186105776 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329658 13194139535070 true] [17592186105776 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329679 13194139535070 true] [17592186105777 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329665 13194139535070 true] [17592186105777 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329672 13194139535070 true] [17592186105777 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329686 13194139535070 true] [17592186105777 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329693 13194139535070 true] [17592186105966 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535070 true] [17592186105966 1100 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t) = 7\\sin ti + 24\\cos tj$ for $[MATH: t ∈ [0, 2π).]$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$n = (a(t)i + b(t)j)/(\\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid)$.\nFind the function $b(t)$.\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" 13194139535070 false] [17592186105966 1100 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" 13194139535070 true] [17592186105966 1145 "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step $2.$\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" 13194139535070 true] [17592186105967 1017 #edb/ref 17592186102049 13194139535070 false] [17592186105967 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329638 13194139535070 true] [17592186105967 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535070 true] [17592186105967 1100 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $x^{2} + y^{2} = 9$. Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$." 13194139535070 false] [17592186105967 1100 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ Find the outward-pointing unit norma